# /{{VERB_PRIME}} — Session calibration ({{ORG_NAME}})

This harness is neutral. **`{{VERB_PRIME}}` is what makes it know how {{ORG_NAME}} works** —
it reads the living process from the wiki so the rest of the session behaves
correctly without anything being hardcoded here.

Run this before `{{VERB_REFINE}}`, `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}`, or any real work.

## Invocation

```
/prime [ticket-key]
```

The optional ticket key narrows live state to one work item. You don't have to
declare a project — `{{VERB_PRIME}}` resolves scope for you (see step 0).

## What this skill does

`{{VERB_PRIME}}` is lightweight by default. Read tier-1 only up front (operating-model,
schema/CLAUDE.md, compliance, the constitution-class ADRs) and pull live tracker state.
Defer deep per-project reads, and promote to that detail only when a later step needs it:
every token read here is carried for the rest of the session (progressive disclosure,
ADR-0003).

Scope is ambient context, not a prerequisite. Org is the default and first-class scope,
so you don't force the user to define a project. Resolve scope in step 0 first; the
reads after it are independent of each other, and steps 7–8 apply in project mode only.

### 0. Resolve scope — Org (default) or a specific project

Before reading anything heavy, resolve "am I on a project, or not?" yourself. Check
these sources in this order of precedence, and stop at the first that answers:

- the **session ticket** (the `[ticket-key]` passed, if any) — its project/component;
- the **repo** you started in (its remote / its local `CLAUDE.md`);
- the **wiki** (project hub entries, if scope is named there);
- a **handoff** note from a prior session (resume state);
- the **tracker** itself (the ticket's project field / parent epic).

Rules:

- Default to Org when no source names a project, or when the work is cross-cutting,
  SRE, or a one-off. Org is the normal case, not "unfiled" or a last resort.
- Resolve scope without forcing a project declaration: use the sources above rather
  than stopping to make the user pick one.
- Escalate to a human only when scope is genuinely ambiguous and no source answers,
  and even then proceed in Org mode unless told otherwise.

Record the resolved scope; you will state it in the calibration summary (step 9) and it
gates steps 7–8 below.

### 1. Locate the wiki

```bash
WIKI=${{{ORG_WIKI_PATH_ENV}}:-{{ORG_WIKI_DEFAULT_PATH}}}
test -f "$WIKI/CLAUDE.md" || { echo "wiki not found at $WIKI — clone {{ORG_WIKI_REMOTE}} or set ${{ORG_WIKI_PATH_ENV}}"; exit 1; }
B=$(git -C "$WIKI" branch --show-current 2>/dev/null)
[ -z "$B" ] || [ "$B" = main ] || echo "$WIKI is on branch '$B', not main: the reads below see unmerged content and the wiki is not syncing. Fix: git -C \"$WIKI\" switch main"
```

If that prints a branch warning, pass it on to the engineer in the summary; don't switch
the branch yourself, since it may hold their unmerged work.

### 2. Read the operating model — the flow you will enforce

```bash
cat "$WIKI/operating-model.md"
```

This defines the {{VERB_REFINE}} → {{VERB_EXECUTE}} → {{VERB_GATE}} flow and who owns each step. It is the
process itself, and it can change here without a harness release.

### 3. Read the schema + hard rules

```bash
cat "$WIKI/CLAUDE.md"
```

The wiki's own schema: how knowledge is structured and the hard
rules (code-as-truth, PR-driven edits, capture-mid-work, no PHI/PII/secrets).

### 4. Read the compliance guardrails

```bash
test -f "$WIKI/compliance.md" && cat "$WIKI/compliance.md"
```

Regulated-context guardrails. If {{ORG_NAME}} ships under a regulated regime, this
governs what you may write, where records must live, and when to stop and ask a
human.

### 5. Read the relevant ADRs

```bash
ls "$WIKI/decisions/"
# Read the ones relevant to this work. The constitution-class ADRs
# (development practices, autonomy posture, disclosure discipline) apply to
# every session — read those always.
```

